 "sentences": [
  "[curious] He wants a two-hundred-and-eighty-dollar party for each kid. She says they can't do both this month. Who's right?",
  "Dan thinks fair means the same for both. Priya thinks fair means what they can actually afford.",
  "They take home ninety-eight hundred a month. Sixty-six hundred of it is bills.",
  "[thoughtful] The fight isn't really about the party. It's about not knowing what the party does to the rest of the month.",
  "So on payday, savings goes first. Eight hundred dollars.",
  "The other twenty-four hundred becomes one number for each day: about eighty dollars. So they know before they book anything.",
  "The ten-year-old's party is two-eighty. That's two hundred over for the day. DivvyUpp spreads it across the next week, so each day drops to about fifty.",
  "Eleven days later, the six-year-old's party fits the same way. Same party for both kids, and the eight hundred is still saved.",
  "[warm] So who's right? Both. Both kids get the party Dan wanted, and the month still works, the way Priya needed. Which one are you?",
  "Save first. Then spend what's safe. Try it at Divvyup dot com."
 ],
